7. Cross-Device Lock Synchronization Logic

A. Global Synchronization Toggle

Add a setting:

Settings → Sync Lock Across Devices

Provide an ON/OFF switch.

When ON:

- The device can send and receive eligible synchronized lock commands.
- Commands can reach other linked devices that also have synchronization enabled.

When OFF:

- The device operates independently.
- It must not receive or execute remote lock commands.
- Its local lock systems must continue working normally.

B. Per-Lock Synchronization Choice

Inside the setup interface of Instant Lock, Delayed Lock, and Schedule Lock, provide:

- All Linked Devices
- This Phone Only

Use This Phone Only as the safe initial default.

If the user selects All Linked Devices, send the command only to eligible linked devices whose synchronization is enabled.

Clearly show the selected mode before starting the lock.

C. Consistent Timer Synchronization

Do not independently restart the full duration on each receiving device.

Every lock command should contain sufficient timing information, including:

- Unique "commandId".
- Origin device ID.
- Lock mode.
- Creation timestamp.
- Intended start timestamp.
- Free-usage end timestamp when applicable.
- Lock end timestamp.
- Relevant schedule or occurrence ID.
- Expiration information.
- Schema version.

Use consistent absolute timestamps so all receiving devices calculate the same remaining duration.

For example:

Delayed Lock

- Free Usage Duration: 20 minutes.
- Lock Duration: 30 minutes.
- Free-usage end: T + 20 minutes.
- Lock end: T + 50 minutes.

Every receiving device must use the same deadlines instead of starting its own independent 20-minute countdown after receiving the command.

Do not send timer updates every second. Synchronize the command and deadlines, then maintain an accurate local timer on each device.

D. Duplicate Prevention

Use command IDs and persistent processed-command tracking.

A repeated Firestore event must never activate the same lock twice.

The application must handle:

- Duplicate snapshots.
- Listener reconnections.
- Delayed command delivery.
- Device restarts.
- Network interruptions.
- Commands arriving out of order.

E. Offline and Late Command Handling

If a device is offline when a command is issued:

- When it reconnects, check whether the command is still valid.
- If a valid lock is still active, apply only the remaining duration.
- For Delayed Lock, if the free-use deadline has passed, begin the remaining restricted-lock period.
- If the entire lock session has already expired, discard the stale command.
- Never restart an expired lock with its original full duration.

Persist active session state locally so temporary connectivity problems cannot interrupt an already active lock.

F. Conflicting Commands

Handle simultaneous commands from multiple devices deterministically.

A new or delayed remote command must never silently shorten, bypass, or weaken a stricter active local lock.

Use a documented conflict policy that preserves the safest valid active restriction or queues a compatible subsequent command.

Make command processing idempotent and prevent race conditions.

G. Schedule Lock Synchronization

When a user creates a Schedule Lock for All Linked Devices, synchronize the schedule configuration to eligible devices.

Each device should activate the corresponding scheduled occurrence only once.

Use a stable schedule ID and unique occurrence ID.

Preserve the intended schedule time zone and recurrence behavior. Handle daylight-saving changes where relevant.

Each device should maintain the necessary local scheduling and reboot-restoration behavior.

A device that is offline should not execute an expired schedule occurrence when it reconnects.

