PART C — FIREBASE SECURITY & RELIABILITY

8. Firestore Security Rules

Inspect and finalize the existing "firestore.rules".

The rules must ensure:

- Unauthenticated users cannot access private user data.
- Only the authenticated owner can access "users/{userId}".
- A user cannot read or modify another user's profile, devices, presets, or lock commands.
- Incoming documents contain only permitted fields and valid data types.
- Command payloads are validated.
- Device and command documents cannot be used to access another account.
- No unnecessarily broad read/write permission exists.

Use strict owner validation through the authenticated Firebase UID.

Never use public read/write rules in production.

Test rules with Firebase Emulator Suite before deployment wherever available.

Consider Firebase App Check if it is compatible with the project's actual distribution and testing environment. Do not enable enforcement blindly if it could block legitimate installations.

Never put service-account credentials, private signing keys, or other server secrets inside the Android application.

9. Cloud Listener & Resource Management

Use efficient Firestore listeners rather than unnecessary repeated polling.

Listeners should:

- Start only when the account and synchronization settings permit them.
- Be detached when the user signs out or disables synchronization.
- Recover gracefully after network reconnection.
- Avoid duplicate subscriptions.
- Avoid unnecessary database reads and writes.

Keep cloud data minimal and synchronize only meaningful changes.

Preserve local operation regardless of cloud state.

